WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:20.070
<v Ira Glass>When she was a kid LaDonna Powell would go to Easter parties at her mom's work. It was great, because her mom worked at JFK airport in New York City. LaDonna and her brothers and sisters and cousins would gather Easter eggs out on a parking lot by the tarmac in what's now the American Airlines terminal. Planes were taking off and landing right overhead. They liked that.
<v Ira Glass>LaDonna Powell小时候常去她妈妈工作的单位参加复活节聚会。那感觉很棒，因为她妈妈在纽约肯尼迪机场工作。LaDonna和她的兄弟姐妹及堂表亲们会在停机坪旁的一个停车场捡复活节彩蛋，那里现在是美国航空公司的航站楼。飞机就在头顶起降。他们喜欢那样。

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:49.970
<v Ira Glass>A bunch of her family works at JFK. Her mom makes airplane meals, but there's also her uncle, two aunts, three cousins, and her sister. Everybody has a role. They feed the passengers, they deliver their bags, they clean up the planes after them. And when she was 20, LaDonna found her role-- she'd keep the airport safe. She got a job with TSA. She scanned bags and laptops. She took it seriously. Like, she would picture the pipeline that runs below the airport, carrying millions of gallons of jet fuel, blowing up if there were any kind of bomb.
<v Ira Glass>她家有好几个人都在肯尼迪机场工作。她妈妈负责做飞机餐，此外还有她叔叔、两个阿姨、三个堂表亲和她妹妹。每个人都有自己的职责。他们为乘客供餐、运送行李、在乘客下机后清理机舱。20岁时，LaDonna找到了自己的角色——保障机场安全。她进入美国运输安全管理局（TSA）工作。她负责扫描行李和笔记本电脑。她非常认真。比如，她会想象机场下方那条输送数百万加仑航空煤油的管道，如果发生任何爆炸，后果不堪设想。

00:00:49.970 --> 00:01:14.480
<v LaDonna Powell>Like, if the bomb goes off in this airport, what happens to everyone here, everyone that I care about if we don't commit and do our job properly here? It was like, I used to have this terrible dream of a woman walking up to me and pressing, igniting a bomb. Every time-- I would go to sleep and have this dream all the time. So when I came to work, I was always very adamant on staring at the X-ray machine.
<v LaDonna Powell>比如，如果这个机场发生爆炸，如果不尽职尽责，这里的所有人，我所关心的人会怎样？我以前常做噩梦，梦见一个女人走近我，然后按下引爆器。每次睡着都会做这个梦。所以上班时，我总是非常执着地盯着X光机。

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:42.260
<v Ira Glass>After a few years at TSA, she moved to a different job, this one out on the tarmac with a private security firm that works at the airport, a firm that eventually became part of Allied Universal, which is a huge company-- the largest security firm in North America, with 150,000 employees all across the country. At JFK, Allied guards screen the vehicles and the airplane catering trucks and all the other stuff that's down there on the runway. LaDonna was excited to work with them. She went through training and then was assigned to shadow a more senior guard.
<v Ira Glass>在TSA工作几年后，她换了一份工作，是在停机坪上一家为机场服务的私营安保公司。该公司后来成了Allied Universal的一部分，这是一家庞大的公司——北美最大的安保公司，在全国拥有15万名员工。在肯尼迪机场，Allied的保安负责检查车辆、飞机配餐车以及跑道上的所有其他物资。LaDonna很兴奋能加入他们。她通过了培训，然后被指派跟随一名资深保安实习。

00:01:42.260 --> 00:02:03.280
<v LaDonna Powell>My first day in the field was not great. I was put with this guy. He was an older white guy. And he was very nice to me but very mean to the people we were screening going onto the runway, because a lot of the duty-free trucks come through that load the planes with food for first class, and he was saying the nastiest things to them.
<v LaDonna Powell>我在现场的第一天表现并不好。我被分配给那个家伙。他是一个上了年纪的白人。他对我很客气，但对我们要检查进入跑道的人却非常刻薄，因为很多免税品卡车都要经过那里，为头等舱装载食物，他对这些人说的话非常难听。

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:10.430
<v Ira Glass>Quick warning that we've un-beeped the curse words here on the podcast. If you prefer a beeped version of our show, maybe you're listening with kids, it's at our website.
<v Ira Glass>友情提示：本播客未对脏话进行消音处理。如果您更喜欢消音版，或者是在有孩子的情况下收听，请前往我们的网站。

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:32.320
<v LaDonna Powell>He was saying all types, like things hurry the f-- hurry the fuck up, move the fuck on, get out of his fucking face. He would try to get free things. So he would, like, threaten them. Like, oh, I'm not going to let you through if you don't give me free food. And they would give him water and juices and stuff. So it's like he was extorting them, basically. Yeah.
<v LaDonna Powell>他什么都说，比如“快点，他妈的快点”，“赶紧滚开”，“离他妈的远点”。他还试图索要免费的东西。他会威胁他们，比如，哦，如果你不给我免费的食物，我就不让你通过。他们只好给他水、果汁之类的东西。所以这基本上就是敲诈。是的。

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:55.700
<v LaDonna Powell>He kept telling me he was a Russian badass. That's what he kept saying. He was like, I'm a Russian badass. And he had-- it was like a baseball cap flipped backwards. We're supposed to wear it forward, with the-- and he flipped it backwards, and he opened his shirt, and he was saying, I'm a Russian bad boy. You know I'm a badass, right? And I was like, I have no idea who you are. Today's my first day, I don't know who this guy is. He's freaking me out already.
<v LaDonna Powell>他一直告诉我他是个俄罗斯硬汉。他反复这么说。他说，我是个俄罗斯硬汉。他戴着一顶反戴的棒球帽。我们本应把帽檐朝前戴，但他把它反过来了，他还敞开衬衫，说，我是个俄罗斯坏小子。你知道我是个硬汉，对吧？我说，我根本不知道你是谁。今天是我第一天上班，我不知道这家伙是谁。他已经把我吓坏了。

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:29.580
<v Ira Glass>After checking cargo vans and trucks for a while, the guy told LaDonna that he was going to be inside the security booth by himself for a bit. She should stay outside by herself and wait. He played music in the booth and started looking at what LaDonna says was a Playboy magazine. LaDonna had read the employee handbook and knew that both of those things were a violation of company policy. And she had also read a book that her best friend gave her, Nice Girls Don't Get the Corner Office. And she thought of it now, the way that guy dismissed her, treated her like, you know, a girl.
<v Ira Glass>在检查货车和卡车一段时间后，那个人告诉LaDonna，他要一个人在安保亭里待一会儿。让她自己留在外面等。他在亭子里放音乐，还开始看LaDonna所说的那种《花花公子》杂志。LaDonna读过员工手册，知道这两项行为都违反了公司规定。她还读过好友送她的书《好女孩进不了转角办公室》。她现在想到了这一点，那个家伙轻视她、把她当成“女孩”对待的方式。

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.280
<v LaDonna Powell>Like I'm overly nice and easy to walk over. Like, OK.
<v LaDonna Powell>就像我很软弱，容易被欺负。好吧。

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:58.780
<v Ira Glass>Wrong. Nice Girls Don't Get the Corner Office spends a lot of time on not being easy to walk over, on the need to speak up, on the need to stand up for yourself. LaDonna reads lots of these business books and management and self-help books. She watches talks online-- not for motivation. She is so motivated, in general, one of her friends calls her Olivia Pope-- like, always wanting to fix something.
<v Ira Glass>错了。《好女孩进不了转角办公室》花了很多篇幅讲不要轻易受人摆布，要勇于发言，要坚持立场。LaDonna读了很多商业、管理和励志书籍。她还在网上看讲座——不是为了激励，她本身就很有动力。她的一个朋友称她为“奥利维亚·波普”（Olivia Pope）——总是想要解决问题。

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:25.070
<v Ira Glass>She reads this stuff because the worldview in these books matches the way that LaDonna already sees things. Like, they give her words to describe what she is feeling all the time anyway-- always try to elevate, always be moving ahead. And this dude, disrespecting his job and her, he was an affront to that entire worldview. So even though it was her very first day out in the field doing her brand-new job, right then, on the tarmac, she decided she had to confront the problem.
<v Ira Glass>她读这些书是因为书中的世界观与LaDonna原本的看法相符。它们给了她描述自己感受的词汇——总是试图提升，总是不断前进。而这个家伙，不尊重工作，也不尊重她，是对她整个世界观的侮辱。所以，尽管这是她到现场开展新工作的第一天，就在那一刻，在停机坪上，她决定必须面对这个问题。

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:46.980
<v LaDonna Powell>So I was riding back with him in the car, and I said to him, why do you do things like that at work? And he was just like, he hates the job. He was like, he hated that place, and that's just what he does. He does whatever he wants to do. And I was like, well, you know, you make it bad for everyone else. If you don't want to be here, just don't come. And then he started calling me, like, Sister Souljah, and then he made a joke about it. And that was it.
<v LaDonna Powell>我和他一起开车回去时，问他，为什么在工作时要那样做？他只是说，他讨厌这份工作。他说，他讨厌那个地方，这就是他的行事风格。他想干什么就干什么。我说，好吧，但你让其他人都很难做。如果你不想待在这，就别来。然后他开始叫我“苏贾修女”（Sister Souljah），还开了一个玩笑。事情就是这样。

00:04:46.980 --> 00:05:20.850
<v Ira Glass>She was not diminished. LaDonna had no respect for a person who did not value this place or this job. And when they got back to the supervisor's office-- LaDonna did not feel great about this, but she told a supervisor about the Russian badass and his behavior. In other words, she handled it. Spoke her mind to the dude, check. Reported him, check. He did not get to her, check and checkmate. And eventually-- she explained this to our producer, Chana Joffe-Walt, who was the one who actually talked to her-- she explained that the Russian badass, he got fired-- for something totally unrelated, actually.
<v Ira Glass>她没有被吓倒。LaDonna对这种不珍惜这里或这份工作的人毫无敬意。回到主管办公室后，LaDonna虽然心里不舒服，但还是向主管报告了那个“俄罗斯硬汉”的行为。换句话说，她处理了这件事。当面说出了自己的想法，搞定；举报了他，搞定。他没能影响她，完胜。最终——她向我们的制作人Chana Joffe-Walt解释道，是Chana亲自和她谈的——她解释说那个俄罗斯硬汉被解雇了——其实是因为一件完全无关的事。

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.890
<v LaDonna Powell>He stole water from Port Authority, and they fired him.
<v LaDonna Powell>他从港务局偷水，他们就开除了他。

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What do you mean he stole water?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你说他偷水是什么意思？

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.920
<v LaDonna Powell>The water coolers, how they have-- he would take them out of the storage and put them in the trunk of his car.
<v LaDonna Powell>饮水机用的那种水，他从仓库里拿出来，放进自己车的后备箱里。

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:30.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[笑声]

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.740
<v LaDonna Powell>And they caught him-- exactly. And they caught him.
<v LaDonna Powell>他们抓到了他——没错。他们抓到了他。

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.440
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Those jugs that go in a water cooler?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>就是那种放在饮水机上的大桶水？

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:43.220
<v LaDonna Powell>Yes. They had him on CC camera going to his car with the water, because he's a badass. He probably walked out with it on his head and carried it to the car.
<v LaDonna Powell>是的。闭路电视拍到他搬着水去车上，因为他是个“硬汉”。他可能顶着水桶走出去，然后搬到车里。

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:48.270
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So he got fired for stealing water, not for looking at pornography while he was on the job?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以他是因为偷水被开除的，而不是因为在工作时看色情内容？

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:49.340
<v LaDonna Powell>Right. Yeah.
<v LaDonna Powell>对。是的。

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:50.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Dude likes water.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这家伙还真是喜欢水。

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.610
<v LaDonna Powell>Yeah, exactly. Clearly.
<v LaDonna Powell>是的，没错。显而易见。

00:05:53.610 --> 00:06:23.330
<v Ira Glass>LaDonna Powell is not a Russian badass. She's a totally different kind of badass. It's like she's a dedicated student of how to be a great employee, the kind of employee who excels and rises in an organization. And now she was entering a company that would be the greatest sort of challenge for an employee like that, a place where people are not playing by the rules. The Russian was just the very first obstacle she faced. It was about to get way uglier.
<v Ira Glass>LaDonna Powell不是什么俄罗斯硬汉。她是另一种硬汉。她是一个如何成为优秀员工的勤奋学生，那种在组织中出类拔萃、不断晋升的员工。现在，她进入了一家对这样的员工来说是最大挑战的公司，一个人们不按规矩办事的地方。那个俄罗斯人只是她面临的第一个障碍。事情只会变得更糟糕。

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:44.430
<v Ira Glass>What you're about to hear is the story of a willful and principled person who sets out to figure out, first, how to rise in this company and, second, how to remake the way the entire place runs, which requires some ingenious strategizing on her part. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>你即将听到的是一个意志坚定且有原则的人的故事，她决心先弄清楚如何在这家公司晋升，其次是如何重塑整个地方的运营方式，这需要她运用一些巧妙的策略。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。请继续收听。

00:06:44.430 --> 00:07:06.710
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Old Guard. Well, Chana Joffe-Walt has been talking to LaDonna, plus lots of other people at Allied Universal at JFK, for months, and now she's going to take over telling LaDonna's story. Just a heads up that there is provocative language and content in here that might not be right for every listener. Here's Chana.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，旧势力。Chana Joffe-Walt几个月来一直与LaDonna以及肯尼迪机场Allied Universal的许多其他人交谈，现在她将接手讲述LaDonna的故事。请注意，内容中可能包含一些挑衅性的语言和内容，可能不适合所有听众。请听Chana的讲述。

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:37.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When you board a plane, sit down, and look out the window, you see the guys throwing the bags, and then, beyond that, just concrete expanse. That's where LaDonna worked. That's where the Allied guards are, stationed in booths along the tarmac. The booths are basically checkpoints for everything that goes out there-- food, fuel, water, duty-free purses. The booths are about the size of a U-Haul trailer, metal and steel with big glass windows.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当你登机坐下向窗外看时，你会看到搬运工人在扔行李，再远一点就是混凝土空地。那就是LaDonna工作的地方。那里有Allied的保安，驻扎在停机坪沿线的岗亭里。岗亭基本上是所有出入物资的检查站——食物、燃料、水、免税包包。岗亭大约有一个U-Haul拖车那么大，是金属和钢材结构的，有巨大的玻璃窗。

00:07:37.480 --> 00:08:05.520
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>At the beginning of each eight-hour shift, LaDonna would go to the Allied locker room, change, and stand in line for roll call outside the supervisor's office. Then the supervisor would tell her which posts she had that day out on the tarmac, and she'd head out there to a booth in the pavement. The guard she was relieving would drive away. Trucks would come by. She would check for bombs, drugs, food packages to see if anything was open. And she would wait-- lots of waiting.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>每八小时轮班开始时，LaDonna会去Allied的更衣室换衣服，然后在主管办公室外排队点名。主管会告诉她今天在停机坪的哪个岗位，她就会前往岗亭。接替她的保安会开车离开。卡车会过来。她要检查炸弹、毒品、食品包裹，看看有没有被打开过。然后就是等待——漫长的等待。

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:13.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Occasionally, a supervisor would come by to check on her. One night, it was an older, gruff supervisor named Kevin McNamara. LaDonna says he came in to her booth--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>偶尔，主管会过来查看她。一天晚上，来了一位粗鲁的老主管Kevin McNamara。LaDonna说他进了她的岗亭——

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:25.390
<v LaDonna Powell>And he asked me to open my jacket. And he took his phone out to take a photo of me. And I was like, excuse me. He was like, open your jacket and turn around. And I was like, I'm not.
<v LaDonna Powell>他让我打开外套。还掏出手机要给我拍照。我说，不好意思。他说，打开外套转一圈。我说，我不。

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:52.750
<v LaDonna Powell>And he tried to say, oh, I'm just taking a picture of you. The company has a policy where we take a photo of you. And I remember reading it when they gave us a handbook, and I said, no, you're supposed to take a picture of my outside, my outer garment. And he's like, no, you're going to open your coat and turn around. He was so abrupt and aggressive with me-- just, you're going to do what I say. Like, you're going to turn around, you're going to do what I say, and that's it. And I was like, that has nothing to do with the job.
<v LaDonna Powell>他试图说，哦，我只是在给你拍照。公司有规定要拍你的照片。我记得在他们发手册时读到过，我说，不，你们应该拍的是我的外侧，也就是外衣。他说，不，你要打开外套转一圈。他对我非常生硬和咄咄逼人——就是，你必须按我说的做。就像，你得转一圈，你得听我的，就这样。我说，这跟工作毫无关系。

00:08:52.750 --> 00:09:13.600
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>The job was to guard the post. LaDonna felt like Kevin wanted a show. LaDonna is small, just under 5 feet tall. Her resting position is almost always perfect posture, standing at attention, hands clasped at her belt, elbows out. The booth was a very tight space for two people.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>这份工作是守岗。LaDonna觉得Kevin想要看表演。LaDonna个子小，不到5英尺高。她平时总保持着完美的站姿，立正，双手交叠在皮带上，手肘向外。岗亭对两个人来说是非常狭小的空间。

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:30.560
<v LaDonna Powell>And I was like, you're going to get the fuck out of my face. Like, I'm not turning around, and I know that's not what you're supposed to be doing, and I'm not supposed to open my clothes for you. And he told me, oh, if I didn't listen, he was going to make it hard. He's like, you always have something to say. You always have something to say.
<v LaDonna Powell>我说，你给我离远点。我不会转圈，我知道那不是你应该做的，我也不应该为你打开衣服。他告诉我，如果不听话，他会让我很难做。他说，你总是那么多话。你总是那么多话。

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:46.260
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>But then he dropped it. He drove away. LaDonna took note. Success-- she drew a line, and he backed down.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>但随后他作罢了。他开车离开了。LaDonna记下了。成功了——她划定了一条底线，他退缩了。

00:09:46.260 --> 00:10:16.280
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>JFK airport covers 5,000 acres. There are approximately 300 Allied guards. LaDonna met some of her coworkers in the locker room before and after shifts. That's where she learned that one of the worst posts was called Post Hotel. It got the most traffic. Second worst was Post Papa. It was distant and remote, though I don't think that's how it got its name. LaDonna noted that nobody wanted these posts, and she also noticed that she started to be assigned them, shift after shift.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>肯尼迪机场占地5000英亩。大约有300名Allied保安。LaDonna在更衣室遇到了她的一些同事。她在那里得知，最差的岗位叫“酒店岗”。那里人流量最大。第二差的是“爸爸岗”。它偏远，虽然我不认为这就是它得名的原因。LaDonna注意到没人想要这些岗位，也注意到她开始被反复指派到这些岗位，班次接上班次。

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:40.940
<v LaDonna Powell>You feel forgotten, in a way, almost, because how do you go to the bathroom? How do you-- you can't do anything. You're just there. At first, it was like, OK, solitude, you know? But then it's like, all right, are you guys coming to relieve me? Can I go to the ba-- can I eat something? Am I going to get to go home? Sometimes, you'll be stuck there 12 hours. They'll forget to send you a relief.
<v LaDonna Powell>你会有种被遗忘的感觉，因为怎么上厕所？怎么……你什么都做不了。你就在那。起初，感觉就像是，好吧，独处，你知道吗？但后来就会想，好吧，你们打算来接班吗？我能去……我能吃点东西吗？我能回家吗？有时，你会连续待上12个小时。他们忘了派人来接你。

00:10:40.940 --> 00:11:04.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There are cameras in the booths so supervisors in the main office can check on their guards. Of course, she couldn't see the supervisor's office, but they could see her. Were they watching her? When she did get a response on the radio, the supervisor would say they didn't have anyone available to relieve her. She'd squirm, needing to go to the bathroom. Did they see that?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>岗亭里有摄像头，主管办公室的主管可以查看保安的情况。当然，她看不到主管办公室，但他们能看到她。他们在监视她吗？当她通过对讲机得到回应时，主管会说没人能接替她。她急得想上厕所。他们看到了吗？

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:18.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Most nights, Kevin was her tour supervisor. He worked in the supervisor's office with the other supervisor on duty. Sometimes he'd drive around to check on people. She'd wonder, are they're busy? Are they not there? Or maybe--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>大多数晚上，Kevin是她的巡视主管。他和当班的另一位主管在主管办公室工作。有时他会开车四处巡视。她会想，他们很忙吗？他们不在吗？或者……

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:37.550
<v LaDonna Powell>Are they picking on me? Am I not getting to go to the bathroom because I spoke up? Am I not getting a lunch break, are my hours being cut because I spoke up about what's happening? And I would always have these thoughts, are they doing this because I keep talking back?
<v LaDonna Powell>他们是在针对我吗？是因为我说了话才不让我去厕所吗？是因为我说了话，我的午休被取消、工作时间被削减了吗？我总是会有这些想法，他们这么做是因为我不断回嘴吗？

00:11:37.550 --> 00:12:07.820
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>When traffic was low, LaDonna would sometimes take out her reading, and suddenly a voice would come into the booth, put that away. Or when she'd eat, no eating in the booth. So they were there. But then, silence for hours. As families took off in planes above her for Florida and business people came in from Dubai, LaDonna was stuck in a box below, amid 5,000 acres of pavement.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>当交通量较小时，LaDonna有时会拿出书来读，突然对讲机里传来一个声音，收起来。或者当她吃东西时，说不准在岗亭里吃。所以他们一直在那里。但随后又是数小时的沉默。当一家家人的飞机从她上方起飞前往佛罗里达，商务人士从迪拜抵达时，LaDonna被困在下面一个盒子里，置身于5000英亩的停机坪中。

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:34.260
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>In the locker room, another guard told LaDonna, there's a corner of the booth where they can't see you. The cameras don't reach there. They can only see your feet. So LaDonna started packing peanut butter and jelly triangles and standing in that corner to eat them. Sometimes that would work. Sometimes she'd hear the supervisor, Kevin's voice suddenly, come forward in front of the camera.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>在更衣室里，另一名保安告诉LaDonna，岗亭有一个角落他们看不到。摄像头够不到那里。他们只能看到你的脚。于是LaDonna开始带花生酱果酱三明治，站在那个角落吃。有时管用。有时她会突然听到主管Kevin的声音，让她到摄像头前面来。

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:45.330
<v LaDonna Powell>And I kept trying to understand, what am I doing wrong with my interactions with him? Because someone else tried to tell me, oh, he's nice to this girl. She's Spanish. It's not because you're black. I--
<v LaDonna Powell>我一直试图理解，我和他的互动到底哪里做错了？因为有人试图告诉我，哦，他对那个女孩很友善。她是西班牙裔。不是因为你是黑人。我……

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He's a white guy?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他是白人吗？

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:57.890
<v LaDonna Powell>He's an older white man. It's something else. And I said, OK, maybe my approach is wrong with him. So I didn't know how to manage him. He was just-- he would have these outbursts.
<v LaDonna Powell>他是位上了年纪的白人。这是别的原因。我说，好吧，也许我对他的方式不对。我不知道该如何应付他。他只是……他会突然发火。

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:11.790
<v LaDonna Powell>He would always say, oh, he's management, and we're not like him, and he's better than the rest of us because he gets an expense card and he wears the blazer. He can do what he wants. Oh, I've been here for years. It's like, how do you manage that? How do I correct you?
<v LaDonna Powell>他总是说，哦，他是管理层，我们和他不一样，他比我们剩下的人都强，因为他有报销卡，还穿西装。他可以想干什么就干什么。哦，我在这里工作很多年了。就像，怎么对付这种人？我该怎么纠正你？

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:46.620
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>Kevin McNamara did not want to talk to us for this story. LaDonna says it was around this time that she started to repeat phrases to herself from the book Managing Your Manager by Gonzague Dufour. It lays out three strategies for handling a bully boss. Number one, don't take it personally. Two, find the humor. And three, most important, limit the pain, target the game. Done correctly, you build skills, you level up, and you move on to a place where you'll get a better boss. View the bully as temporary.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>Kevin McNamara拒绝接受我们的采访。LaDonna说，大约从那时起，她开始对自己重复Gonzague Dufour所著《管理你的经理》中的名句。书中提出了处理霸道老板的三种策略。第一，不要个人化。第二，寻找幽默感。第三，也是最重要的一点，限制痛苦，锁定目标。操作得当，你会积累技能，升级，然后跳槽到有更好老板的地方。把霸道老板视为暂时的。

00:13:46.620 --> 00:14:10.930
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>This is how she would correct Kevin-- do her job extremely well, get through this part, and be seen by the people above Kevin. She'd repeat to herself, everyone has a boss. And it was that boss-- Kevin's boss-- she was focused on. At the worst post, on the worst shift, without breaks, LaDonna targeted the game. It was busy, lots of opportunities for growth.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>这就是她纠正Kevin的方式——把工作做到极致，熬过这一段，被Kevin之上的人看到。她对自己重复，每个人都有老板。而她关注的正是那个老板——Kevin的老板。在最差的岗位、最差的班次，没有休息时间，LaDonna锁定了目标。那里很忙，有很大的成长机会。

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:29.670
<v LaDonna Powell>I really learned a lot, just working out there. You're outside in the field, so it's very hands-on. You're using a mirror, you're checking under vehicles for bombs, you're checking under vehicles for any anomalies, anything that's not supposed to be there, just making sure when it goes on the runway that it's safe. So it felt good, purposeful. Like, OK, I'm actually doing something.
<v LaDonna Powell>我在外面工作确实学到了很多。你在现场，所以非常务实。你需要用镜子，检查车辆下是否有炸弹，检查车辆下是否有任何异常，任何不该在那里的东西，只是确保在进入跑道时它是安全的。所以感觉很好，很有目标感。就像，好吧，我真的在做点什么。

00:14:29.670 --> 00:15:00.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This appeared to go unseen by Kevin. Everyone has a boss, she'd tell herself, but she never heard from Kevin's boss. What continued to be noticed was her failure to put her body where the supervisors could see it. She'd hear their voices in the booth, telling her to come forward in front of the camera. When she worked hard, nothing. When she needed a bathroom relief after 8 or 10 or sometimes 12 hours, no answer. So she'd limit the pain. On her way to work--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Kevin似乎没看到这一点。每个人都有老板，她对自己说，但她从未收到Kevin老板的反馈。他们继续关注的是她未能将身体置于主管能看到的地方。她会在岗亭里听到他们的声音，叫她站到摄像头前面。她努力工作时，没人理。当她工作8或10甚至12小时后需要上厕所时，没人应答。所以她会限制痛苦。在上班路上——

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:10.080
<v LaDonna Powell>I would go to the-- there was a convenience store on Lefferts Boulevard before I got there, and I would go to the convenience store. I would get a juice or ask for a coffee and get an extra cup on the bottom of it.
<v LaDonna Powell>我会去……在到那之前，Lefferts大道上有一家便利店，我会去那里。我会买果汁或要杯咖啡，在杯子底下再放一个空杯。

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:17.860
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>In the locker room, before her shift, LaDonna would throw in the cup with the rest of her stuff for when she had to pee. She'd head to roll call, with Kevin.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>在更衣室，轮班前，LaDonna会把这个杯子和她的其他东西放在一起，以备她需要尿尿时使用。她去点名，和Kevin在一起。

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:36.980
<v LaDonna Powell>And I would be there. I would have a roll of tissue-- like, I'd roll it up on my hand in the locker room-- and an empty cup with me all night. Oh, and that was another thing. He would come and look at your bag, because you have a clear plastic bag. They want to make sure you're not carrying any electronics out there onto the field, or you're not stealing anything. So you have a transparent bag. OK, fine.
<v LaDonna Powell>我就在那。我会带一卷纸巾——在更衣室里卷在手上——还有个空杯子和我一起待一整晚。哦，还有件事。他会过来检查你的包，因为你有一个透明塑料袋。他们想确保你没有带任何电子产品到现场，或者没有偷任何东西。所以你有一个透明袋。好吧，行。

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:52.420
<v LaDonna Powell>You can see straight through my bag, but then he still would make comments about my pads and my tampon-- oh, why do you need so many pads? It's like, why do you care? It doesn't bother you, and clearly you can see that it's not something I stole from here, so why do you care?
<v LaDonna Powell>你可以直接看穿我的包，但他还是会对我的卫生巾和卫生棉条发表评论——哦，你为什么需要这么多卫生巾？这就像，你关心这个干嘛？这又不打扰你，而且很明显这不是我从这偷的，所以你关心这个干嘛？

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:57.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Everyone has a boss, she told herself.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>每个人都有老板，她对自己说。

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:06.850
<v LaDonna Powell>Don't go back at him. Tone it down and say, OK. Just try to focus on me. How can I get past this stage?
<v LaDonna Powell>不要回嘴。压低声音说，好的。尽量专注于我自己。我该如何度过这个阶段？

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:43.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>LaDonna stuck to her plan, did her job extremely well, worked hard. And then, just as the book said, she was noticed-- not by Kevin or his boss but by a manager at the airport. This man had seen LaDonna working, seen how hard she worked, and he recommended to Allied that they promote LaDonna to supervisor. This recommendation likely carried weight-- the airport is Allied's client-- and it definitely had an effect on LaDonna. She'd never even thought about being a supervisor. That was many levels above what she'd imagined.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>LaDonna坚持她的计划，工作非常出色，非常努力。然后，正如那本书所说，她被注意到了——不是被Kevin或他的老板，而是被机场的一位经理。这个人看到LaDonna在工作，看到她有多努力，于是建议Allied将LaDonna提升为主管。这个建议可能很有分量——机场是Allied的客户——这对LaDonna确实产生了影响。她甚至从未想过要当主管。那比她想象的级别高多了。

00:16:43.640 --> 00:17:25.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And then she read Lean In. Lean In is by Sheryl Sandberg, the COO of Facebook. It explains why so few women make it to the top of their professions. Sandberg argues that women-- and this is the line LaDonna slowed down on-- women systematically underestimate their abilities. LaDonna had been so focused on her bosses underestimating her abilities, maybe she had underestimated herself. Now she'd been noticed. Someone saw her as supervisor material. She just had to answer one question for herself, the question posed by Sheryl Sandberg, one of the most powerful female executives in the world-- could she picture herself as a boss?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>然后她读了《向前一步》（Lean In）。《向前一步》的作者是Facebook首席运营官谢丽尔·桑德伯格。它解释了为什么很少有女性能做到职业生涯的顶峰。桑德伯格认为女性——LaDonna在这一行停了下来——女性系统性地低估了自己的能力。LaDonna一直专注于她的老板低估她的能力，也许她低估了自己。现在她被注意到了。有人认为她是主管人选。她只需要为自己回答一个问题，这个问题由世界上最强大的女性高管之一谢丽尔·桑德伯格提出——她能想象自己当老板吗？

00:17:25.340 --> 00:17:50.270
<v LaDonna Powell>It was like, do I really want to be in charge of other people? And then I have to take a step back and say, do I really want these kind of people in charge of me? It's like, I never had that moment until then. These are the guys in charge of me? I'm peeing in a cup outside. These are the people in charge of me. So I really had to evaluate what I was doing-- what I was doing mentally to myself. Like, I was literally my biggest enemy.
<v LaDonna Powell>就像，我真的想负责管理其他人吗？然后我必须退后一步说，我真的想让这种人管我吗？就像，在那之前我从未有过这种时刻。这些人是管我的？我在外面用杯子尿尿。这些人是管我的。所以我真的必须评估我在做什么——我在精神上对自己做了什么。就像，我真的是我最大的敌人。

00:17:50.270 --> 00:18:05.670
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>That voice in the booth, the unseen authority that LaDonna had found so unnerving, suddenly seemed ridiculous. Why is he counting her pads? Why can't she just eat her PB and J? They seemed desperate and threatened.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>岗亭里的那个声音，LaDonna觉得令人不安的那个隐形权威，突然显得很可笑。他为什么要数她的卫生巾？她为什么不能吃她的花生酱三明治？他们看起来绝望又受到威胁。

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:29.580
<v LaDonna Powell>I can do the job you're doing. You're not Jesus right now because you're a supervisor. I can read and execute this job just like you, if not better, because all you're doing is acting like Adolf Hitler, an insane person here, and you're treating people like animals. Let's try the other way.
<v LaDonna Powell>我能做你做的工作。你现在不是耶稣，因为你是主管。我能读懂并执行这份工作，就像你一样，甚至更好，因为你所做的只是像阿道夫·希特勒，一个精神病患者一样在这里行事，把人当动物对待。让我们换种方式。

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:47.460
<v Chana   Joffe-Walt>After two years at Allied, LaDonna made it to supervisor. She went from $15 an hour as a guard to $32 an hour. And in June 2014, she entered the supervisor's office.
<v Chana   Joffe-Walt>在Allied工作两年后，LaDonna晋升为主管。她的时薪从15美元涨到了32美元。2014年6月，她进入了主管办公室。

00:18:47.460 --> 00:19:11.530
<v LaDonna Powell>I get to walk in, and now they're going to see me in my blue shirt-- because the supervisors wear a different blue shirt from the security guards-- and I was like, oh, they're going to piss their pants. They're going to be so pissed off because now I'm equal. We're both in the same field. We're both in the same position. You won't call me a bitch. You won't do that because now, possibly, you might view me as an equal.
<v LaDonna Powell>我走进去，现在他们会在我穿着蓝色衬衫时看到我——因为主管穿的蓝色衬衫与安保人员不同——我想，哦，他们会吓尿的。他们会气疯的，因为现在我是平等的。我们都在同一个领域。我们都在同一个职位。你不会叫我婊子。你不会那样做，因为现在，可能，你会视我为平等的。

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:56.770
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The supervisor's office was about the size of a large bedroom, an open space with three desks, computers, a printer-copier machine, and schedules all over the walls. There were about 12 other supervisors, different races, mostly men. They worked in shifts of two. LaDonna met her new boss-- Kevin's boss, the boss of all the supervisors-- a tall black man named Chris Timberlake. And she was given 50-something guards of her own to manage each shift, guards who needed schedules and overtime arrangements and to be counted at the beginning and end of every shift. She managed payroll and drove the premises to check on the guard booths. She went to daily supervisor briefings. Every part felt urgent and exciting, especially the meetings, where they talked strategy.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>主管办公室大约有一间大卧室那么大，是一个开阔的空间，有三张桌子、电脑、一台打印复印机，墙上贴满了时间表。还有大约12位其他主管，不同种族，大多是男性。他们两人一组轮班。LaDonna见到了她的新老板——Kevin的老板，所有主管的老板——一位名叫Chris Timberlake的黑人高个子。她被分配了50多名保安供她在每个班次管理，这些保安需要排班、加班安排，并且需要在每个班次开始和结束时点名。她负责工资发放，开车巡视岗亭。她参加主管每日简报。每一部分都让人感到紧迫和兴奋，尤其是会议，他们在会上讨论战略。

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:01.830
<v LaDonna Powell>Sometimes I can't wait for them to finish a sentence. I just jump in. I said, sometimes, I feel like they're thinking slower than me.
<v LaDonna Powell>有时我迫不及待地想让他们说完一句话。我就直接插进去。我说，有时，我觉得他们思考得比我慢。

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:02.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[笑声]

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:08.050
<v LaDonna Powell>Like maybe they're still figuring it out, and I'm already at the end point. And I'm like, well, that's not going to work.
<v LaDonna Powell>就像他们可能还在摸索，而我已经到了终点。我说，好吧，那行不通。

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:20.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>LaDonna had new ideas. She was nice, but not too nice, assertive, but deferential to her boss Chris Timberlake, bringing things to him first. Like, one time, she went to his office across the hall--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>LaDonna有新想法。她很友善，但不太软弱；很自信，但对她的老板Chris Timberlake毕恭毕敬，先和他沟通。比如，有一次，她去了走廊对面的办公室——

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:32.480
<v LaDonna Powell>And I said to him, we have a training class. I'm just talking to him-- now, I'm a supervisor at this time-- and he's like, come here, come around the desk for a second. I want to show you something on the computer. I'm like, OK, what's going on?
<v LaDonna Powell>我对他说，我们要上培训课。我只是在和他说话——那时我是主管——他说，过来，到桌子这边来一下。我想在电脑上给你看点东西。我说，好吧，怎么了？

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:52.470
<v LaDonna Powell>So I come around to look. I'm like, hey-- and it's women pole dancing, and they're, like, licking on each other in a strip club and pole dancing. He's like, can you do this? And he's calling the other male sup-- come here, come here, let me show you what I'm showing Powell. And they're both showing it to me. He's like, yeah, I seen that. I seen that on YouTube. It's crazy, right? Powell, you could do that, right? You could do that? You do that at home?
<v LaDonna Powell>我走过去看。我说，嘿——那是女人在跳钢管舞，她们在脱衣舞俱乐部里互相舔舐，跳着钢管舞。他说，你能这样吗？他还叫其他男主管过来，过来，看看我给Powell看的东西。他们都在给我看。他说，是啊，我看了。我在YouTube上看的。太疯狂了，对吧？Powell，你能那样，对吧？你能那样吗？你在家里那样吗？

00:20:52.470 --> 00:21:00.460
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In LaDonna's head, she's Sheryl Sandberg right here. They were imagining her in a porn show, pole dancing.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在LaDonna脑海里，她现在就是谢丽尔·桑德伯格。他们把她想象成在色情表演中跳钢管舞。

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:09.010
<v LaDonna Powell>And I was like, you know what? Fuck the both of you. And I just walked out. I just walked out. I was like, you two are the worst, the worst type of people. I walked out.
<v LaDonna Powell>我说，你知道吗？去你们俩的。我直接走了出去。我就是走了出去。我说，你们两个是最坏的，最糟糕的人。我走了出去。

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:10.800
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Did you say that to them, or did you just--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你对他们说了吗，还是你只是……

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.760
<v LaDonna Powell>I said that to them, and they just laughed me off. They laughed me off.
<v LaDonna Powell>我对他们说了，他们只是把我当作笑话。他们只是嘲笑我。

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:54.790
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>So it wasn't just Kevin. It was Kevin's boss too-- Chris Timberlake, who was now her boss. He told LaDonna, you have nice lips, made comments about her body. When she'd go to the copier, he'd come up behind her and push into her. Chris Timberlake, like Kevin McNamara, did not want to talk to us for this story. LaDonna says she'd come in to work to find other supervisors gathered around someone's phone, watching videos of people having sex. They'd invite her over and ask her if she could do it like that.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>所以不只是Kevin。还有Kevin的老板——Chris Timberlake，他现在是她的老板。他告诉LaDonna，你有漂亮的嘴唇，还对她的身体发表评论。当她去复印时，他会走到她身后蹭她。Chris Timberlake和Kevin McNamara一样，拒绝接受我们的采访。LaDonna说，她上班时会发现其他主管围在某人的手机旁，看着人们做爱的视频。他们会叫她过去，问她能不能那样做。

00:21:54.790 --> 00:22:07.800
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>LaDonna surveilled the supervisors in the same way they used to surveil her. She studied how they operated and just how closely they watched the women who worked there. Like with one guard, Marsha-Nique Irving.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>LaDonna以他们曾经监视她的方式监视着这些主管。她研究他们的运作方式，以及他们监视在那里工作的女性的程度。比如有个保安，Marsha-Nique Irving。

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:14.110
<v LaDonna Powell>She would change her hair often or her makeup often, and they would make sexual comments about it.
<v LaDonna Powell>她经常换发型或换妆容，他们会对此发表性评论。

