<v Chana Joffe-Walt>比如什么？

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:35.100
<v LaDonna Powell>Like, oh, you saw Marsha-Nique? Her titties look good today, and she got her hair done. Or she would change her lashes-- like, they were longer. Like, oh, she got porno-star lashes, things like that. And she would always brush them off. And she was very feisty, and I loved that-- I loved to watch her answer them. And it was like, good for you. You know? It was like, good for you.
<v LaDonna Powell>比如，哦，你看到Marsha-Nique了吗？她今天的乳房看起来不错，她还做了头发。或者她换了睫毛——比如，更长了。比如，哦，她做了色情明星睫毛，之类的。她总是把这些当耳旁风。她非常有活力，我喜欢这一点——我喜欢看她回应他们。感觉就像，做得好。你知道吗？就像，做得好。

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:52.130
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>LaDonna was working one night, and she heard Marsha-Nique radio in to her supervisor, Osvaldo Ortiz-- everyone called him Ozzie. LaDonna was doing paperwork. Ozzie was sitting with another male supervisor, monitoring the guard radios. And Marsha-Nique radioed to say she needed bathroom relief.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有一天晚上LaDonna在上班，听到Marsha-Nique用对讲机呼叫她的主管Osvaldo Ortiz——大家都叫他Ozzie。LaDonna正在处理文书工作。Ozzie和另一位男主管坐在一起，监控着保安对讲机。Marsha-Nique用对讲机说她需要上厕所。

00:22:52.130 --> 00:23:27.960
<v LaDonna Powell>And I watched him tell her four times, oh, give me a few minutes. I'm trying to find somebody. They were never trying to find somebody. Never once did they call anybody else, ask anybody else, check a sheet to see if you had somebody else. The two men that were in there was like, fuck her, she always talking shit, fuck her. And it made me look up, because I was doing paperwork. And I turned, and I was like, this is what they were saying about me when I was asking to use the bathroom. And it was so much validation. My assumptions were validated. It was like, wow, this is really what you guys are doing.
<v LaDonna Powell>我看着他四次告诉她，哦，给我几分钟。我正试着找人。他们根本没想找人。他们从未呼叫过别人，问过别人，查看过有没有人可以替班。屋里的那两个男的就像是，去他妈的，她总是废话多，去他妈的。这让我抬头看，因为我当时在做文书。我转过身，心想，这就是我申请上厕所时他们说的话。这简直是太多的验证了。我的猜想被验证了。就像，哇，这就是你们这些家伙在做的事。

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:35.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This went on for hours. LaDonna didn't say anything at first. She just watched, as Marsha-Nique radioed again.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这种情况持续了几个小时。LaDonna起初什么也没说。她只是看着，听着Marsha-Nique再次呼叫。

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:58.600
<v LaDonna Powell>Calling to go to the bathroom-- and she said to them, I have my period. You could tell she was already hesitant to say it, because she's saying it on a live stream. Not only can all of us hear it-- which is like 57 radios of people-- now you're making her say that, and the client can also hear her. So that's our 57 radios and eight radios Port Authority in Manhattan and Port Authority in JFK can hear.
<v LaDonna Powell>呼叫去厕所——她对他们说，我来月经了。你能看出她本来就很犹豫要不要说，因为她是在直播频道说的。不仅我们所有人都能听到——大约有57个对讲机的人——现在你让她说那个，客户也能听到她。所以这是我们的57个对讲机，加上曼哈顿和肯尼迪机场港务局的8个对讲机都能听到。

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:13.350
<v LaDonna Powell>Embarrassing. She ended up using the bathroom on herself and bleeding on herself that day. Nothing, no sorry. They were all laughing about it, thought it was hilarious. Oh, she going to sit in her piss until she gets off. Like, they had no concern for her.
<v LaDonna Powell>太尴尬了。那天她最终尿在身上，经血也流在身上。什么也没有，一句道歉都没有。他们都在嘲笑，觉得这很有趣。哦，她会坐在自己的尿里直到下班。就像，他们对她毫无关心。

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:30.370
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The supervisor, Ozzie, did not want to talk to us. I did talk to Marsha-Nique. She describes this the same way LaDonna does. But of course, at the time, Marsha-Nique couldn't see what was happening in the supervisor's office, the things LaDonna was now seeing clearly.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>主管Ozzie拒绝接受我们的采访。我和Marsha-Nique谈过。她描述的情况和LaDonna一样。但当然，那时Marsha-Nique看不到主管办公室里正在发生什么，看不到LaDonna现在看得一清二楚的事。

00:24:30.370 --> 00:24:42.150
<v LaDonna Powell>I'm not just paranoid. This is in stereo. Because seriously, I really was like, OK, maybe you're just paranoid. And then you get in the office, and it's like, oh, no. They're disgusting.
<v LaDonna Powell>我不只是多疑。这是立体声的。因为说真的，我以前真的会想，好吧，也许你只是多疑。然后你进了办公室，就是，哦，不。他们真恶心。

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:52.580
<v LaDonna Powell>It's just disgusting. This is what you're doing at work-- at work. I think they need a shirt that says "At Work." Maybe they don't get it. We are at work.
<v LaDonna Powell>这真是太恶心了。这就是你们在工作时做的事——在工作时。我觉得他们需要一件写着“在工作”的衬衫。也许他们不明白。我们在工作。

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:56.000
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>It's just like a little T-shirt reminder.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>就像一件小T恤提醒一下。

00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:01.590
<v LaDonna Powell>Right. Every time they start to do it, they're like, oh, wait, at work. Yes, you're at work.
<v LaDonna Powell>对。每次他们开始做这事时，他们就会想，哦，等等，在工作。是的，你在工作。

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:29.120
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>Being at work means something specific to LaDonna, something she assumes is universal but not everyone feels. To her, you're at work. It's almost like saying you're at church. You're supposed to be your best and look out for others.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>在LaDonna看来，在工作意味着具体的东西，一些她认为是普遍但并非每个人都有所感的东西。对她来说，你在工作。就像说你在教堂一样。你应该做到最好，照顾他人。

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:42.760
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>In Lean In, Sheryl Sandberg tells a story. When she was pregnant-- she was working at Google-- it was the early years of the company. And she was late for a meeting. She had to park way far out. The company was growing so fast, there was no parking close by.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>在《向前一步》中，谢丽尔·桑德伯格讲了一个故事。她怀孕时——在谷歌工作——那是公司成立早期。她开会迟到了。她不得不停在很远的地方。公司发展太快，附近没有停车位。

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:59.310
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>And after running, with a big belly, to get to her meeting, Sandberg marched in to speak with Sergey Brin and Larry Page, the founders of Google. We need pregnancy parking, she said. Oh, they said. Sure, of course. They just never thought of it before.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>在挺着大肚子跑去开会后，桑德伯格大步走进办公室，去见谷歌创始人谢尔盖·布林和拉里·佩奇。我们需要孕妇停车位，她说。哦，他们说。当然，没问题。他们只是以前从未想过。

00:25:59.310 --> 00:26:15.680
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>Sandberg writes, "That is the power of being in the room. The other pregnant women must have suffered in silence. Having one pregnant woman at the top made the difference." That was the power LaDonna felt she had, in the room, when Marsha-Nique called in. And she used it.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>桑德伯格写道：“这就是身处其中的力量。其他怀孕的女性一定在默默承受。有一位怀孕的女性身居高位，结果就不同了。”这就是LaDonna在Marsha-Nique呼叫时，作为办公室一员所感到的力量。她利用了这一点。

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:34.430
<v LaDonna Powell>And when I said it to them, the first time-- like, so she's not going to get to go to the bathroom? No, I'm not going to send that relief over there for her. She'll figure it out. How? There's nowhere for her to go. She's not a-- she can't just pull her penis out and pee in the woods. And then he just-- he was like, this is what she's calling for? And I'm like, you get a period?
<v LaDonna Powell>当我第一次对他们说时——就像，所以她不能去厕所？不，我不会派替班去那。她自己会想办法的。怎么想？她没地方去。她又不是……她不能在大森林里掏出阴茎尿尿。然后他只是……他就像，她就为了这个呼叫？我说，你来过月经吗？

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:49.510
<v LaDonna Powell>And he looks at me. I'm like, you tell me if you get a period, if you know exactly-- a woman doesn't want to keep a pad on more than four hours. And it's that kind of thing that I just wanted to be in the room to be able to say-- consider this person, consider them as a person.
<v LaDonna Powell>他看着我。我说，你告诉我你是否来过月经，如果你完全了解……一个女人不想把卫生巾戴超过四小时。正是那种事，我只想在房间里能够说——考虑一下这个人，把她们当一个人来考虑。

00:26:49.510 --> 00:27:37.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Consider this person-- that is what LaDonna's presence was supposed to do, to induce empathy, the kind of consideration she had wanted from her boss, Kevin, when she was out in the field. LaDonna put herself in the supervisor's room with him, made him see her-- blue shirt, just like him. But LaDonna says when she came in as supervisor, Kevin's response was not to consider her. She says his response was, wow, they must have been scraping the bottom of the barrel to bring you in. LaDonna says Kevin would violate protocol and change the schedule without regard to people's medical needs. LaDonna would correct him. Consider this person. When he told the LaDonna to change the payroll so they wouldn't have to pay one of the guards overtime he was owed, LaDonna held onto the piece of paper and said, no.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>考虑这个人——这正是LaDonna的存在所要达到的目的，去引发同理心，那种她以前在现场时想要从主管Kevin那里得到的尊重。LaDonna把自己和他们放在主管室里，让他看到她——蓝色衬衫，和他一样。但LaDonna说，当她作为主管进来时，Kevin的反应并不是尊重。她说他的反应是，哇，他们肯定是为了招你进来而搜刮了桶底。LaDonna说Kevin会违反规定，不顾人们的医疗需求而随意更改时间表。LaDonna会纠正他。考虑这个人。当他告诉LaDonna更改工资单以便不支付一名保安应得的加班费时，LaDonna拿着那张纸说，不。

00:27:37.330 --> 00:28:08.620
<v LaDonna Powell>And then he took the paper, and he threw it back in my face. And he was like, you're always talking back. You're always talking back. It was like, I just-- I didn't know what to say. And then he was like I'm tired of seeing you niggers' faces, and he walked out the room. That was just like, wow. So now we know why you feel like I'm the bottom of the barrel and those kind of things and I'm disgusting. So thank you for letting me know.
<v LaDonna Powell>然后他拿过那张纸，扔回我的脸上。他说，你总是回嘴。你总是回嘴。我就是……我不知道该说什么。然后他说，我厌倦了看到你们这些黑鬼的脸，然后他走出了房间。那感觉就像是，哇。所以现在我们知道为什么你觉得我是桶底之货，觉得那些，觉得我很恶心了。所以谢谢你让我知道。

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:21.690
<v LaDonna Powell>I remember being so enraged. I was like, there's got to be a law against this. And I remember there was a book with all the codes and everything from the company. And I went through the book and I found discrimination, I found hostile work envir-- ugh, I'm sorry.
<v LaDonna Powell>我记得当时非常愤怒。我想，一定有法律可以禁止这种事。我记得公司有一本包含所有准则的书。我翻遍了那本书，我发现了歧视，我发现了敌对工作环境……呃，抱歉。

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:23.770
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>That's OK.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>没关系。

00:28:23.770 --> 00:29:03.140
<v LaDonna          Powell>And I wrote it up. And I wrote the pages, and I did all this stuff, and I wrote a four-- I wrote a complaint. It was, like, four pages long. I turned it in to the woman who was in human resources. I gave it to the man-- I hand-delivered it to them. I can't experience this anymore. Like, I'm tired of working under Kevin. I said, every time I'm by myself with him, he finds a way to tear me down, talk to me disgusting. I'm tired of it. Forever I have to experience this, just because I stood up for myself? It's just not fair.
<v LaDonna          Powell>我写下来了。我写了几页，我做了所有这些事，我写了一个四……我写了一份投诉。足足四页长。我把它交给了人力资源部门的那个女人。我把它给了那个男人——我亲手交给了他们。我再也无法忍受这种经历了。就像，我厌倦了在Kevin手下工作。我说，每次我和他单独在一起，他就会找机会拆我台，说话真恶心。我厌倦了。仅仅因为我坚持了自己的立场，就必须永远经历这些？这太不公平了。

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:15.490
<v LaDonna          Powell>They gave me the most nonchalant response. Oh, Powell, you're reading too much into it. I told you before, Kevin's just like that. If you can't handle the position in there, let us know. We'll put you back in the field.
<v LaDonna          Powell>他们给了我最冷漠的回答。哦，Powell，你想得太多了。我以前告诉过你，Kevin就是那样。如果你应付不了那个职位，告诉我们。我们会把你调回现场。

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:55.550
<v Chana   Joffe-Walt>Everyone has a boss. LaDonna complained about Kevin to their boss, Chris Timberlake, and to his boss, project manager Martin Feeney. Martin Feeney also declined to talk to us. There was a union for guards, but LaDonna wasn't a guard anymore. She was management. According to Allied protocol, every employee complaint triggers an investigation. LaDonna says nobody followed up with her about what happened with Kevin beyond that one conversation with HR and Martin Feeney, which she says ended with Martin Feeney telling her, kick him in the balls next time.
<v Chana   Joffe-Walt>每个人都有老板。LaDonna向他们的老板Chris Timberlake，以及项目经理Martin Feeney投诉了Kevin。Martin Feeney也拒绝接受我们的采访。保安有一个工会，但LaDonna不再是保安了。她是管理层。根据Allied规定，每位员工的投诉都会触发调查。LaDonna说，除了那次与人力资源部门和Martin Feeney的谈话外，没人跟进Kevin的事，那次谈话以Martin Feeney告诉她“下次踢他蛋蛋”而告终。

00:29:55.550 --> 00:30:15.040
<v Chana   Joffe-Walt>LaDonna knew this strategy already. She knew it didn't work. When she was 19, working at Applebee's, she says a coworker grabbed her ass and said he liked Jamaican bodies. She turned around and smashed him in the face with a big metal spoon. Didn't help. LaDonna had tried all the things. They weren't working.
<v Chana   Joffe-Walt>LaDonna已经知道这个策略了。她知道没用。她说她在Applebee's工作时，19岁，一个同事抓了她的屁股，说他喜欢牙买加人的身体。她转过身，用一把大金属勺子砸了他的脸。没用。LaDonna试过了所有办法。都没用。

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:45.950
<v LaDonna Powell>And no matter how far I leaned into my career-- I made it-- I'm the first female to ever start there as a guard at my age and make it to a supervisor. I leaned all the way in without letting anyone touch me, with just showing I was capable-- I could read, I could type, I was able, I understood the job. I made it all the way to supervisor. Still, I'm still being harassed. It just didn't work. It worked as far-- OK, now I've excelled in my career, but I'm still being harassed. Now what? Now what do we do?
<v LaDonna Powell>无论我如何在职业生涯中投入——我做到了——我是第一个以那个年纪从保安做起并升至主管的女性。我全身心投入，没有让任何人碰我，只是表现出我的能力——我能读、能写，我有能力，我了解工作。我一路晋升为主管。但我还是受到了骚扰。这根本没用。这只管用到……好吧，现在我在事业上出类拔萃了，但我还在受到骚扰。现在该怎么办？现在我们该做什么？

00:30:45.950 --> 00:31:08.400
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>LaDonna settled on a new idea for how to fix it all at once. She could see clearly how the male supervisors were treating the guards now that she was on the inside. She could also suddenly see the female guards and how they were responding. She was in the office every day with another male supervisor, and the women guards would show up in there.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>LaDonna想出了一个新主意，该如何一劳永逸地解决问题。现在她身在内部，能清楚地看到男主管是如何对待保安的。她也能突然看到女保安是如何回应的。她每天都和另一位男主管在办公室里，女保安会到那里来。

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:28.680
<v LaDonna Powell>They come in, they're bringing him lunch, shirt open, breasts out-- oh, hi. So some of them were benefiting, using it as a tool to get what they wanted out of it. And watching them with the men supervisors, it was like, why are you doing that? But they're doing it for a good post, for overtime, for a better break.
<v LaDonna Powell>她们走进来，给他带午餐，衬衫敞开，乳房露着——哦，嗨。所以她们中的一些人从中受益，把它当作工具来获取她们想要的东西。看着她们和男主管在一起，就像，你为什么那样做？但她们是为了好岗位、加班费、更好的休息时间而做的。

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:42.650
<v LaDonna Powell>It's like, don't do that. Just don't do it. If we all would say no, it would make it easier for all of us saying no. But you saying yes makes it harder for me saying no. Let's just say no, because we're at work. Let's say no.
<v LaDonna Powell>就像，别那样做。就是别做。如果我们都拒绝，那么所有人都拒绝会更容易。但你说了“是”，让我说“不”变得更难。我们一起说“不”吧，因为我们在工作。让我们说“不”。

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:52.300
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>This was it. When LaDonna heard Allied needed someone to train the new recruits, newly-hired guards, she immediately volunteered herself.
<v Chana  Joffe-Walt>就是这个。当LaDonna听到Allied需要人来培训新员工时，她立刻主动请缨。

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:54.620
<v LaDonna Powell>Let me be the first face they see.
<v LaDonna Powell>让我成为他们看到的第一个面孔。

00:31:54.620 --> 00:32:29.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>These people are new. She could teach them. This was her new idea. She would change the entire culture of the place by starting at the beginning, when people were hired. She would train them correctly. She could teach the women not to respond to sexual advances. She would teach them to focus on work, security. And all the new hires would take root in this place, like an invasive species. They would change the entire ecology. The supervisors would not respond to LaDonna telling them to change, so she'd change the world around them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这些人是新手。她可以教他们。这是她的新主意。她要从头开始，在人们被录用时就重塑整个地方的文化。她会正确地培训他们。她可以教女性不要回应性骚扰。她会教她们专注于工作、安全。所有的应聘者都会像入侵物种一样在这个地方扎根。他们会改变整个生态。主管们不会理会LaDonna让他们改变的建议，所以她会改变他们周围的世界。

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:41.340
<v Ira  Glass>Chana Joffe-Walt. Coming up, the re-education campaign begins. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira  Glass>Chana Joffe-Walt。稍后，再教育运动开始。从芝加哥公共广播电台收听，节目继续。

00:32:41.340 --> 00:33:03.480
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, "LaDonna." We're telling the story of LaDonna Powell and watching as she brings her enormous sense of mission to the job of securing JFK airport in New York, and to making the company that she works for, Allied Universal, a better place to work. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, The New Guard.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是Ira Glass。今天的节目叫“LaDonna”。我们讲述的是LaDonna Powell的故事，看着她如何带着强烈的使命感去确保纽约肯尼迪机场的安全，并努力让她工作的Allied Universal公司成为一个更好的工作场所。我们已经进入节目的第二幕。第二幕，新势力。

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:16.240
<v Ira Glass>So, as you heard in the first half of the show, LaDonna had tried managing up, to reshape her boss's behaviors. Now she went in the opposite direction. And she created something brand new. She was going to start from the beginning, training the new recruits. Again, here's Chana Joffe-Walt.
